{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-descartes-elliptic-invariants","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:descartes-elliptic-invariants","kind":"remark","title":"Invariants and classical limit of $\\mathcal{E}_q$","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-descartes-elliptic-curve"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-descartes-elliptic-curve","tags":["elliptic-curve","descartes","j-invariant","classical-limit","weierstrass"],"text":"descartes-elliptic-invariants.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"rem:descartes-elliptic-invariants\"></a><p><strong>Invariants.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n<th>Value at the substrate parameter <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Discriminant</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Delta(q)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-18.95</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j</code>-invariant</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j(q) = 256(2z^2 + z + 2)^3 / \\Delta</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">129.9</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Real components</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code> (since <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Delta &#x3C; 0</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2-torsion</td>\n<td>one real point at <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">x_T = -p(q)/3 - \\cdots</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">x_T = -0.684</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Classical limit.</strong>\nAt <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q = 1</code> (equivalently <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s = 1</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">z = 0</code>), the curve degenerates to</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  E_1  :  y^2 = x^3 + \\tfrac{2}{3}x + \\tfrac{20}{27},\n  \\qquad \\Delta = -16, \\quad j = 128.</code></pre>\n<p>Clearing denominators (<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">X = 3x</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">Y = 3\\sqrt{3}y</code>) yields the integral model <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">Y^2 = X^3 + 18X + 60</code> with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Delta = -2^4 \\cdot 3^5 \\cdot 31</code>. The factor <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">31</code> reflects the arithmetic of the quintic resolvent underlying Bring's surface.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/descartes-elliptic-invariants.md","heading":"Remark","number":"7.8.20"}
